      "message": "Hmm, late thought: so if we assume that a malicious host may still trigger this issue even for a non-hotpluggable device (in particular, for a device in SATC), e.g. by abruptly resetting or powering-down the device, it means it can also be triggered by userspace, via sysfs? So userspace \n\nHopefully this is not a big problem: looks like files like `/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/reset`, `/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/config` etc are only writable by root.\n\nHmm, OTOH `/sys/bus/pci/devices/*/remove` is writable also by users in the `root` group.\n\nI\u0027ve just tried \"removing\" the GPU via this `remove` file. It succeeded - the device got removed (even though obviously it is not hotpluggable, and it is in SATC). It immediately triggered a kernel panic in the GPU driver, unrelated to pKVM. But I suppose theoretically it could trigger the hard lockup addressed here as well?",
